1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,660
Alaska, the frozen frontier, where ice and snow cloak huge deposits of oil.

2
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:14,980
1973, oil shortage, gas crisis, and a nation desperate for Alaskan crude.

3
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:24,060
The risky solution, a pipeline across 800 miles of pristine wilderness, active fault lines, and a hostile terrain.

4
00:00:24,061 --> 00:00:30,060
Now, the challenge of the Alaskan oil pipeline on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:47,780
This is what 75 degrees below zero looks like above the Arctic Circle.

6
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:53,100
When temperatures drop this low, living, merely being, becomes a struggle.

7
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:57,800
Exposed flesh freezes and dies in ten minutes.

8
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:01,060
Fingers freeze solid in only 90.

9
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:05,660
I took my mitten off and instantly wished I hadn't.

10
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:08,700
The cold was horrible and sank through my skin,

11
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:12,360
freezing in place all the nerves and tendons beneath it.

12
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:14,640
The limb was soon useless to me.

13
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:19,260
The viscous fluid of my eyes felt as though they were freezing over

14
00:01:19,261 --> 00:01:21,420
and turning to glass.

15
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:23,440
Diary entry.

16
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:27,060
an arctic explorer.

17
00:01:27,061 --> 00:01:31,060
In 1958, two oil companies seemingly defied common sense

18
00:01:31,061 --> 00:01:36,060
by exploring for oil in this harsh and unforgiving place.

19
00:01:36,061 --> 00:01:40,060
Ten years later, the two oil companies, Humble Oil, now Exxon,

20
00:01:40,061 --> 00:01:43,060
and Atlantic Richfield, announced they have discovered

21
00:01:43,061 --> 00:01:47,060
the largest oil field in North America.

22
00:01:47,061 --> 00:01:51,060
The oil is in Alaska, just beneath its Prudhoe Bay,

23
00:01:51,061 --> 00:01:54,220
and there are close to 10 billion barrels waiting for them.

24
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:59,200
A staggering total of over 420 billion gallons of fuel.

25
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:03,860
The area around Prudhoe Bay is known as Alaska's North Slope,

26
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,040
located deep within the Arctic Circle.

27
00:02:06,041 --> 00:02:10,120
It is a timeless wilderness that has gone virtually untouched by man.

28
00:02:10,980 --> 00:02:14,300
The nearest human contact is some 500 miles away.

29
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:18,380
At the same time as public awareness of environmental issues grows,

30
00:02:18,381 --> 00:02:25,340
so does pressure on lawmakers to begin creating legislation that will protect the environment from its most willful offenders.

31
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:27,680
Often, that was big industry.

32
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:31,600
Events came to a head when a nationwide rally is called.

33
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,960
It became known as Earth Day.

34
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:36,560
Twenty million people demonstrated.

35
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:42,180
This was the first time the public had an opportunity to express their concern

36
00:02:42,181 --> 00:02:45,160
about the deterioration of the environment,

37
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:50,080
which the political establishment hadn't been paying any attention to.

38
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:54,580
And for the first time, the environmental movement as a whole began to take on muscle.

39
00:02:54,581 --> 00:03:00,380
So they were well prepared at the time of the discovery of oil in Prudhoe Bay in 1968

40
00:03:00,381 --> 00:03:01,680
to take it head on.

41
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:05,080
The people had spoken and the government began to listen.

42
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:09,040
In Washington, plans were to establish a watchdog organization.

43
00:03:09,041 --> 00:03:12,660
an environmental protection agency began to take shape,

44
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:15,480
along with a host of landmark legislation

45
00:03:15,481 --> 00:03:18,840
setting federal standards for clean air and clean water.

46
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:22,040
The oil companies announced that they intended

47
00:03:22,041 --> 00:03:24,380
to build an 800-mile-long pipeline

48
00:03:24,381 --> 00:03:27,020
that would cut a path through the very heart of the state,

49
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:28,800
southward to Valdez.

50
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:32,220
To environmentalists, crude could be applied

51
00:03:32,221 --> 00:03:34,060
to the whole unseemly plan,

52
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:36,980
not just to what would be flowing through its steel pipe.

53
00:03:36,981 --> 00:03:45,960
By the end of 1968, groups such as the Friends of the Earth and the Wilderness Society vowed to bring a halt to the proposed project.

54
00:03:45,961 --> 00:03:51,920
We took the last great place and we put this thing across it.

55
00:03:52,760 --> 00:04:05,760
And that is an indication of our casual contempt for something as important, as fragile, as ecologically irreplaceable as the great heart of Alaska.

56
00:04:05,761 --> 00:04:14,400
But the oil companies involved in exploring the North Slope moved quickly to form a partnership to share in the huge expense of this mammoth undertaking.

57
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:21,580
The partners were now known collectively as Alyeska, the given name for Alaska by its Alleyud Indians.

58
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:24,020
It translates as Great Land.

59
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:30,440
An order went out for 800 miles of pipeline 48 inches in diameter,

60
00:04:30,441 --> 00:04:37,600
the only size thought capable of withstanding the immense pressure of the 2 million barrels of oil that would be pumped through it each day.

61
00:04:38,340 --> 00:04:44,600
The 550,000 tons of pipeline would have to be shipped on huge barges from steel plants in Japan,

62
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:49,480
at what was considered an exorbitant price, $100 million.

63
00:04:51,860 --> 00:04:55,700
When the cost of the construction eventually soared to over $9 billion,

64
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:58,880
this was considered the pipeline's one and only true bargain.

65
00:04:58,881 --> 00:05:08,160
But with the arrival of pipe supplies and crews also comes lawsuits and injunctions against construction from concerned environmentalist groups.

66
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:13,940
It is a test of the newly signed National Environmental Protection Act, NEPA,

67
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:18,560
which will set legal standards on all projects having an effect on the environment.

68
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:22,940
The freewheeling days of the old oil monopolies were long gone.

69
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:26,520
Oil companies no longer simply got whatever they wished.

70
00:05:26,521 --> 00:05:29,300
The public now had a greater say in the matter.

71
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:39,660
The oil companies did not, in my view, anticipate the vigor of the opposition they would have in building this pipeline.

72
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:43,300
I think they were initially surprised by that.

73
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:50,880
To Alaskan natives and environmentalists, the oil companies seemed less interested in protecting nature than they do their profits.

74
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:55,060
Careful examination will need to be made if the plan is to proceed.

75
00:05:55,061 --> 00:06:09,061
And I think because of the actions of the environmentalists in asserting they would sue unless certain construction design remedies were incorporated into the project,

76
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:19,020
that we would have had a much less successful pipeline venture than we now have.

77
00:06:19,021 --> 00:06:27,740
By April 1970, less than 10 months after ARCO's announcement to build a pipeline, construction stops before it ever truly begins.

78
00:06:28,540 --> 00:06:31,040
Congress and the courts put a halt to the project.

79
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:35,820
The pipeline isn't so much in Alaska as it is in Limbo.

80
00:06:35,821 --> 00:06:47,240
Alaska is a larger-than-life place that has always attracted dreamers and hard-sold men of action,

81
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:50,580
all scheming to make fortunes off its generous lands.

82
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:55,100
The Russians had practiced this philosophy long before America did.

83
00:06:55,880 --> 00:07:01,780
During the late 1700s, Alaska was a part of Russia and served as her base for the booming fur trade,

84
00:07:01,781 --> 00:07:03,980
the richest enterprise in the world.

85
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:08,980
A single sea otter skin could equal three times the average Russian's yearly income.

86
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:15,480
By 1866, Russia was in near financial ruin due to her military humiliation

87
00:07:15,481 --> 00:07:19,820
at the hands of Francis Napoleon III in the Crimean War.

88
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:22,580
Russia needed money. Badly.

89
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:26,960
Knowing this, American Secretary of State William Seward

90
00:07:26,961 --> 00:07:30,680
began urging President Andrew Johnson to purchase Alaska

91
00:07:30,681 --> 00:07:34,680
as a source of great natural wealth for the growing young republic.

92
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:38,280
The price was a steal, $7 million.

93
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:41,520
Broken down, it worked out to two cents an acre.

94
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:44,920
The smart thinkers of the day thought it was too much.

95
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:47,540
Seward was labeled an idiot in the newspapers.

96
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:49,840
They called the treaty Seward's Folly.

97
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:55,320
Seward prevailed and finally convinced Congress to purchase Alaska.

98
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:59,840
By the turn of the century, money was flowing back to the rest of America

99
00:07:59,841 --> 00:08:03,680
in the form of whaling, timber, and the Alaska gold rush.

100
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:09,060
By 1898, more than $300 million in Klondike gold

101
00:08:09,061 --> 00:08:12,120
had been extracted from Alaska's fertile ground.

102
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:15,940
By the year of its admission to statehood in 1959,

103
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:20,480
Seward's Folly had already made a $50 billion return

104
00:08:20,481 --> 00:08:22,380
on the government's initial investment.

105
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:26,700
In another 10 years, that figure would double itself,

106
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:29,540
not in gold, but in black gold.

107
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:40,600
The state of Alaska receives more in oil taxes and royalties every two days than the 7.2 million paid to Russia to purchase the territory.

108
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:45,620
We now return to Modern Marvels.

109
00:08:47,080 --> 00:08:53,860
The history of oil is a happy accident, beginning with the industry's first wildcatter, Edwin Drake.

110
00:08:53,861 --> 00:08:57,220
Edwin Drake was what we would call an entrepreneur.

111
00:08:57,221 --> 00:09:00,680
or in the 19th century, he would be called a fortune hunter.

112
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:02,940
He went out looking for ways to make money,

113
00:09:02,941 --> 00:09:06,580
and essentially he drilled the first modern oil well.

114
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:11,980
Instead of simply mopping up the oil seeps on the surface as others were doing,

115
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:16,300
Drake was the first to see what might lie below the ground with his drill.

116
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:18,960
He used a water drilling technique,

117
00:09:19,140 --> 00:09:21,840
which was basically hammering a pipe into the ground.

118
00:09:21,841 --> 00:09:27,600
Until Drake, people had been using whale oil to light their lanterns.

119
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:31,080
But whales were being hunted down at a tremendous rate.

120
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:34,860
It was a lighting source that was literally doomed for extinction.

121
00:09:35,580 --> 00:09:39,040
A larger, more readily available source for lighting was needed.

122
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:43,260
Drake's investors looked to Titusville, Pennsylvania to make their fortune.

123
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:47,800
Here, oil freely floated up from the earth in a place called Oil Creek.

124
00:09:47,801 --> 00:09:53,000
The local Indians, the Seneca, knew about oil and used it for medicinal purposes.

125
00:09:53,980 --> 00:09:57,660
Later, the white man would bottle the stuff up and call it snake oil,

126
00:09:58,240 --> 00:09:59,900
a play on the word Seneca.

127
00:10:01,080 --> 00:10:04,100
Drake dug deeper, but time and money were running out.

128
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:06,560
The well looked to be a dry one.

129
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:10,160
Drake's investors wanted to pull out and cut their losses.

130
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:14,480
A letter was sent to Drake advising him to pay his remaining bills

131
00:10:14,481 --> 00:10:16,900
and close the operation by the end of the day.

132
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:20,060
Fortunately for Drake, the mail was late.

133
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:23,200
Drake didn't get the letter until the next day,

134
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:26,260
just as oil began flowing from his well.

135
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:30,980
The history of the modern oil industry had been a mere 12 hours away

136
00:10:30,981 --> 00:10:32,540
from being dramatically altered.

137
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:36,180
As for Drake, the Wildcatter title was well earned.

138
00:10:36,181 --> 00:10:39,560
He took all the money that he earned from that very first well

139
00:10:39,561 --> 00:10:42,260
and spent every last dime looking for another one.

140
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:46,540
Drake died 21 years later at the age of 61.

141
00:10:46,900 --> 00:10:48,860
absolutely penniless.

142
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:58,620
Now that Drake had shown that oil could be tapped,

143
00:10:58,860 --> 00:11:02,560
the trick became finding a way to get it from the field into people's homes.

144
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:06,180
Americans were developing a quick addiction to oil.

145
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:11,860
The quantity of oil that was being demanded by consumers was rising very rapidly,

146
00:11:11,861 --> 00:11:17,900
and oil had, for the most part, been shipped literally in barrels.

147
00:11:18,240 --> 00:11:21,260
And then those tanks were being hauled around by wagons

148
00:11:21,261 --> 00:11:23,840
and then that was too slow and not enough

149
00:11:23,841 --> 00:11:26,220
and they started loading this on the railroads.

150
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:31,120
It was because of the railroads that America's first oil pipeline was built.

151
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:34,140
John D. Rockefeller was an oil tycoon

152
00:11:34,141 --> 00:11:38,200
but he also controlled most of the railroads that traveled between Pennsylvania

153
00:11:38,201 --> 00:11:41,660
and the oil-thirsty cities along the eastern seaboard.

154
00:11:41,860 --> 00:11:49,420
Because of this power, Rockefeller was able to dictate what freight fees were charged to his smaller oil producing competitors.

155
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:52,100
Fees that took a big bite out of their profits.

156
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:58,380
Rockefeller's competition organized themselves into a group called the Independents.

157
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:01,820
The Independents seized upon a radical new idea.

158
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:08,440
Build a 100 mile long pipeline and let their fortunes flow virtually to their customers doorsteps.

159
00:12:08,441 --> 00:12:10,920
People really didn't know how to build pipelines either.

160
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:19,360
In the early days of the oil industry, some of the first pipelines were built out of trees that were bored out so that you had wooden pipelines.

161
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:26,860
Then manufactured steel, cast iron pipelines were being used.

162
00:12:27,140 --> 00:12:29,780
But Rockefeller wouldn't go down without a fight.

163
00:12:30,420 --> 00:12:35,980
He delayed their construction any way he could, including blocking the path of their pipeline with his trains.

164
00:12:35,981 --> 00:12:42,640
When that failed to make the competition fold, Rockefeller decided to beat them at their own game.

165
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:46,700
Rockefeller, in fact, began by building a competing pipeline

166
00:12:46,701 --> 00:12:51,400
because he realized that you could not possibly control the production of the oil.

167
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:55,900
But if you control what's called the downstream aspects, the shipping and the refining,

168
00:12:56,400 --> 00:12:57,740
then you would control the market.

169
00:12:58,220 --> 00:13:00,940
So he actually put the independence pipeline out of business

170
00:13:00,941 --> 00:13:03,380
by building a bigger and more efficient pipeline.

171
00:13:03,381 --> 00:13:10,860
The entire fledgling oil industry quickly realized that pipelines were the most efficient way of delivering oil to the public.

172
00:13:11,660 --> 00:13:17,280
By 1880, more than 75% of America's oil was being transported by pipelines.

173
00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:23,640
Sixty years later, another important pipeline would be built to deliver oil to the East Coast.

174
00:13:23,641 --> 00:13:31,300
It was called the Big Inch, and its purpose was to deliver fuel to America's warships as they set sail for battle in Europe.

175
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:38,380
By 1880, oil had gone from being used as a medicine to lighting an entire nation,

176
00:13:38,820 --> 00:13:42,520
but it was soon to be put to an even more extraordinary use.

177
00:13:47,020 --> 00:13:51,420
Edwin Drake had been looking for oil to light lamps by kerosene.

178
00:13:52,120 --> 00:13:56,400
As kerosene is refined, a byproduct is produced, gasoline.

179
00:13:57,240 --> 00:14:00,500
Until the combustion engine was invented in 1892,

180
00:14:00,501 --> 00:14:04,400
gasoline was considered a waste product of no use to anyone.

181
00:14:05,140 --> 00:14:09,420
The oil and automobile industries rapidly changed America's energy needs.

182
00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:12,520
In the first 40 years of the existence of the automobile,

183
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:16,820
there were an astounding 24 million cars in use in the United States.

184
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:21,800
In the post-World War II years, that number increased to 90 million.

185
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:27,100
More than 28 billion gallons of oil were being consumed by Americans every year.

186
00:14:27,101 --> 00:14:31,520
the American love affair with the automobile had firmly taken root.

187
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:36,120
The soldiers came back, they had cheap loans, they started buying things, the economy boomed.

188
00:14:36,260 --> 00:14:40,420
People switched from tanks to cars, they switched from bombers to prefab houses.

189
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:44,420
Levittown, the great suburb, was developed.

190
00:14:44,680 --> 00:14:48,800
The result was that you had a marriage really between the economy,

191
00:14:49,420 --> 00:14:51,940
the automobile industry, which was the biggest industry,

192
00:14:52,120 --> 00:14:55,500
and then the oil industry, which was really pretty much the second biggest industry.

193
00:14:56,140 --> 00:14:59,380
When President Eisenhower initiated the Federal Highway Act,

194
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:03,780
it gave America a modern set of roads that would link it from coast to coast.

195
00:15:04,300 --> 00:15:07,860
Detroit automakers encouraged folks to get out and discover the country,

196
00:15:08,340 --> 00:15:11,340
and Americans did, including Alaska.

197
00:15:12,440 --> 00:15:15,100
Improvements were underway on the Alcan Highway,

198
00:15:15,101 --> 00:15:18,940
the Lower 48's direct link to its new estate via Canada.

199
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:22,740
For some, the Alaskan-Canadian Highway went a long way

200
00:15:22,741 --> 00:15:25,760
to paving the way for the Alaskan oil pipeline.

201
00:15:26,760 --> 00:15:29,940
Certainly construction of the highway, the Alcan Highway,

202
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:33,340
conditioned folk to accept a major project

203
00:15:33,341 --> 00:15:35,920
that would cut across wilderness country.

204
00:15:36,120 --> 00:15:38,900
Some of us, including myself, again felt that

205
00:15:38,901 --> 00:15:42,520
since we already had an incursion into a wilderness area

206
00:15:42,521 --> 00:15:46,420
through the highway, why not put the pipeline parallel down it?

207
00:15:46,421 --> 00:15:50,120
The fortunes of a nation and the oil industry were tied.

208
00:15:50,121 --> 00:15:54,500
They grew together at a one-to-one ratio during those heavy boom years.

209
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:56,860
Oil's influence was felt everywhere.

210
00:15:57,380 --> 00:16:01,780
Cars, appliances, homes, everything ran on the power that oil provided.

211
00:16:02,460 --> 00:16:08,620
And by the late 1960s, America's appetite for oil far outstripped its domestic supply.

212
00:16:09,220 --> 00:16:13,440
Most of the oil consumed by Americans did not come from within their own borders.

213
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:19,540
Dependence on foreign-produced oil from the Middle East had been growing steadily for over a decade.

214
00:16:20,120 --> 00:16:23,060
America needed a solution to its growing problem,

215
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:26,500
and the Alaskan oil pipeline seemed to offer a solution.

216
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:31,660
But with environmental issues still clouding the picture, its future looked bleak.

217
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:39,720
During the 1990s, one-tenth of all oil used by Americans passed through the Alaskan oil pipeline.

218
00:16:41,420 --> 00:16:44,560
We now return to modern marvels.

219
00:16:44,561 --> 00:16:52,940
It wasn't politicians in Washington or engineers in Anchorage that changed the fate of the Alaskan pipeline.

220
00:16:52,941 --> 00:16:58,280
It was the actions taken by a small group of Middle Eastern oil sheiks from OPEC,

221
00:16:58,680 --> 00:17:01,540
the Organization of Oil Exporting Countries.

222
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:06,140
So you had the situation where the oil companies were ready to construct the pipeline

223
00:17:06,141 --> 00:17:09,680
and they faced such strong environmental opposition.

224
00:17:10,180 --> 00:17:11,520
They simply could not proceed.

225
00:17:11,521 --> 00:17:15,960
and then they received the very nice gift of an oil crisis.

226
00:17:16,580 --> 00:17:20,120
Two and three hour lines at gas stations became the norm.

227
00:17:20,700 --> 00:17:24,320
Gas crunch, odd even days and rationing became buzzwords.

228
00:17:24,820 --> 00:17:27,560
In 1973, OPEC was a dirty one.

229
00:17:28,640 --> 00:17:31,660
The oil embargo cemented in everyone's minds

230
00:17:31,661 --> 00:17:34,760
the need to end America's dependence on foreign oil.

231
00:17:35,320 --> 00:17:38,360
The issue was larger than just a lack of cheap gasoline.

232
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:40,880
This did a lot of economic damage.

233
00:17:40,881 --> 00:17:45,399
It's the same as if you have a tax. You take money out of the pockets of consumers.

234
00:17:45,400 --> 00:17:46,760
They can't spend as much.

235
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:50,183
They don't buy things. Factories cut back. Factories lay off workers.

236
00:17:50,184 --> 00:17:51,100
You get a recession.

237
00:17:51,940 --> 00:17:57,320
And the oil crisis was really the end of the post-war economic boom.

238
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:03,360
America's energy demands have grown so rapidly that they now outstrip our energy supplies.

239
00:18:03,361 --> 00:18:11,040
As a result, we face the possibility of temporary fuel shortages and some increases in fuel prices in America.

240
00:18:11,860 --> 00:18:13,140
This is a serious challenge.

241
00:18:13,880 --> 00:18:19,960
If the spigot could be turned on, over 25% of the nation's oil supply would come flowing daily from Alaska.

242
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:29,020
A country gripped by inflation and high unemployment would also gain over 70,000 jobs, an important boom in bad times.

243
00:18:29,740 --> 00:18:32,300
Opposition to the pipeline was quickly fading.

244
00:18:33,360 --> 00:18:41,400
For more than four decades, oil companies had been searching for oil in Alaska.

245
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,040
Its existence had been known for years.

246
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:49,000
The Eskimos, like the Seneca, had been using oil as a cock for their canoes.

247
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:53,420
But with discovery of oil comes equal parts joy and dread.

248
00:18:53,920 --> 00:18:55,880
Oil is many times a mixed blessing.

249
00:18:56,520 --> 00:19:00,000
They've known about oil in Alaska since the 1920s.

250
00:19:00,001 --> 00:19:04,500
there were wells punched, I think, in 1922.

251
00:19:05,380 --> 00:19:07,280
And you can see evidence of that still.

252
00:19:07,281 --> 00:19:13,560
Some of the old trails and drilling devices are still in place.

253
00:19:14,140 --> 00:19:16,660
Exploring for oil had never been a cheap endeavor.

254
00:19:17,480 --> 00:19:22,820
Years of wildcatting often resulted in nothing more than empty hunches and a flatter wallet.

255
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:28,720
Today's average well costs $5 million before any return might be seen.

256
00:19:29,620 --> 00:19:33,460
Oil production in remote areas like the Arctic Circle and the North Sea

257
00:19:33,461 --> 00:19:35,760
were even more expensive to explore.

258
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:40,080
By the 60s and 70s, that was no longer the case.

259
00:19:40,480 --> 00:19:43,200
With demand high and the financial returns great enough,

260
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:46,520
oil companies were stretching their search ever outward.

261
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,700
For one pioneering explorer, British Petroleum,

262
00:19:49,701 --> 00:19:53,120
the wildcatting spirit of Edwin Drake, lived on.

263
00:19:53,121 --> 00:19:59,880
BP had been the sole company looking for oil along Alaska's Arctic coastline

264
00:19:59,881 --> 00:20:04,140
and since 1958 British Petroleum had acquired leases

265
00:20:04,141 --> 00:20:08,400
on hundreds of thousands of state-owned acres around the North Slope.

266
00:20:09,180 --> 00:20:13,020
For ten long years every well that had been dug had come up dry

267
00:20:13,021 --> 00:20:17,840
and by 1968 they had pulled up stakes and abandoned any hope of hitting oil.

268
00:20:18,760 --> 00:20:20,640
Nearby Arco's luck was better

269
00:20:21,080 --> 00:20:25,140
Using a derrick that British Petroleum had left behind, Arco hit oil.

270
00:20:25,720 --> 00:20:28,100
BP's Alaska hunch was right.

271
00:20:28,520 --> 00:20:31,180
They had just been drilling in the wrong places.

272
00:20:31,840 --> 00:20:34,640
British Petroleum came back to the slope in a hurry.

273
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:38,920
They still owned a number of leases, and when the discovery was made,

274
00:20:39,440 --> 00:20:42,700
Arco then volunteered to acquire those leases, and BP says,

275
00:20:42,701 --> 00:20:44,340
no, I think we'll put one more hole down.

276
00:20:44,700 --> 00:20:46,940
And, of course, they hit as well in the Prudhoe Bay area.

277
00:20:47,460 --> 00:20:49,300
An oil fever was raging now.

278
00:20:49,301 --> 00:20:53,060
The remaining state lands in Prudhoe Bay were to be sold at auction.

279
00:20:53,240 --> 00:20:59,780
Total amount of bid, $3,546,292.

280
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:06,820
The same land that had gone for only $12 an acre a year earlier was now fetching well over $2,000.

281
00:21:07,280 --> 00:21:12,740
When the auctioning was complete, Alaska's treasury had grown wealthier by more than $900 million.

282
00:21:12,741 --> 00:21:13,481
dollars.

283
00:21:13,482 --> 00:21:20,520
With the environmentalist voices weakened in light of looming oil shortages,

284
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:24,776
a go-ahead seemed to be drawing nearer for the pipeline.

285
00:21:24,777 --> 00:21:26,800
Engineers and designers started

286
00:21:26,801 --> 00:21:29,500
focusing on the nuts and bolts of production.

287
00:21:29,501 --> 00:21:32,320
And while a pipeline may have been the favorite

288
00:21:32,321 --> 00:21:37,180
plan of the oil companies, they agreed to look at alternate routes due to the pressure

289
00:21:37,181 --> 00:21:38,960
of concerned environmentalists.

290
00:21:39,340 --> 00:21:43,180
The Teamsters wanted to have a big four-lane highway up there

291
00:21:43,181 --> 00:21:44,380
that could haul it by truck.

292
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:48,140
We're talking about hauling it subsurface under the ice by submarine.

293
00:21:48,780 --> 00:21:55,480
All sorts of schemes and plans that were laid one by one to rest mercifully.

294
00:21:56,380 --> 00:21:58,820
One of those was the SS Manhattan,

295
00:21:59,220 --> 00:22:02,140
a double-hulled tanker with ice-breaking capabilities.

296
00:22:02,141 --> 00:22:05,540
It would be a test to see if oil could be moved

297
00:22:05,541 --> 00:22:07,720
through the straits of the icy Bering Sea.

298
00:22:08,560 --> 00:22:11,800
On its journey northward in the summer of 1969,

299
00:22:12,460 --> 00:22:15,820
the Manhattan time and again found itself stuck in ice.

300
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:20,640
The big tanker repeatedly needed to be rescued by the other icebreakers.

301
00:22:21,160 --> 00:22:22,840
At one point during the test run,

302
00:22:22,841 --> 00:22:27,320
a projection of ice managed to rip a giant gash along its thick steel hull.

303
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:29,060
The Manhattan was empty,

304
00:22:29,420 --> 00:22:33,360
but for a ship capable of carrying 700,000 barrels of oil,

305
00:22:33,361 --> 00:22:38,860
it was an ominous note of the potential dangers of shipping oil across the sea.

306
00:22:39,580 --> 00:22:43,400
While ice-breaking tankers like the SS Manhattan were no longer an option,

307
00:22:44,100 --> 00:22:48,500
tankers as a whole would still have a vital role to play in the pipeline system.

308
00:22:49,460 --> 00:22:53,800
By transporting the oil through a pipeline right through the middle of Alaska

309
00:22:53,801 --> 00:22:55,980
to the ice-free port of Valdez,

310
00:22:56,360 --> 00:23:00,760
we would be able to move tankers through that area 24 hours a day, 7 days a week,

311
00:23:00,761 --> 00:23:02,880
without fear of that area freezing up.

312
00:23:02,881 --> 00:23:07,840
So that provided the most economical and most environmentally sound route to follow.

313
00:23:08,620 --> 00:23:12,860
Even with the route chosen, the pipeline faces an even trickier path ahead.

314
00:23:18,360 --> 00:23:25,260
Thousands of lawsuits and environmentalists' court challenges threaten to undermine the project at any step of the way.

315
00:23:25,920 --> 00:23:30,480
Unless they are settled, pipeline construction simply cannot move forward.

316
00:23:31,300 --> 00:23:35,600
Among them are lawsuits brought by Alaska's 200 Indian tribes.

317
00:23:36,360 --> 00:23:43,260
The native claims seek legal assurance that reimbursements will be made for the pipeline's right-of-way through Indian territory.

318
00:23:43,980 --> 00:23:47,440
The fate of the pipeline comes down to one final vote in the Senate.

319
00:23:47,441 --> 00:23:50,600
It is the project's last chance for survival.

320
00:23:51,360 --> 00:23:55,380
One radical sweeping bill can clear-cut the way for the pipeline.

321
00:23:55,381 --> 00:24:09,340
The vote was tied at 48-48, and Vice President Spiro Agnew cast the vote in favor of the Gravel Amendment to cut off further lawsuits.

322
00:24:10,220 --> 00:24:18,660
And that was the bill, the legislation, that in effect settled the issue and allowed the pipeline to go forward.

323
00:24:19,160 --> 00:24:23,620
Interestingly, Agnew's last act as vice president was giving the pipeline its go-ahead.

324
00:24:23,621 --> 00:24:31,220
The 100,000 pieces of pipe that have been gathering dust on Alaskan docks for the past six years are finally going to be put to use.

325
00:24:31,680 --> 00:24:33,156
The work stoppage is over.

326
00:24:33,157 --> 00:24:36,620
The Trans-Alaska Pipeline is about to be officially underway.

327
00:24:37,120 --> 00:24:44,800
And now that the legal obstacles have been overcome, it is time to overcome building on a land known for its devastating earthquakes.

328
00:24:44,801 --> 00:24:51,800
When pipeline construction began, so many people came to Alaska in search of work,

329
00:24:51,801 --> 00:24:56,800
that the state government put ads in newspapers asking people to stay home.

330
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:01,800
We now return to Modern Marvels.

331
00:25:03,800 --> 00:25:08,800
The Alaskan pipeline was an original. There had been nothing like it before.

332
00:25:08,801 --> 00:25:14,780
More than 3 million tons of machinery and supplies had to be delivered north.

333
00:25:15,480 --> 00:25:19,500
Most of it was shipped during the summer months on giant sea barges

334
00:25:19,501 --> 00:25:23,360
to take advantage of the reduced risk of dangerous ice flows.

335
00:25:24,020 --> 00:25:28,720
And that included crew quarters, all prefabricated in the lower 48.

336
00:25:29,480 --> 00:25:36,200
After being tugged into Prudhoe Bay, they would be towed off, moved into place, and welded together.

337
00:25:36,201 --> 00:25:42,740
Building in the harsh Arctic environment would test engineers' technical know-how at every turn,

338
00:25:43,280 --> 00:25:47,400
beginning with nature, that wasn't going to turn over its bounty without a fight.

339
00:25:48,280 --> 00:25:51,960
Its secret weapon was a little-studied phenomenon called permafrost,

340
00:25:52,320 --> 00:25:55,000
soft, silty soil that is infused with ice.

341
00:25:55,700 --> 00:25:59,060
It is any ground that has remained frozen for more than two years.

342
00:25:59,920 --> 00:26:03,640
If it is given the chance to thaw, the soil around it turns to mud,

343
00:26:03,641 --> 00:26:07,880
pipeline with individual segments weighing 9,000 pounds apiece

344
00:26:07,881 --> 00:26:11,200
simply can't survive without stable ground beneath it.

345
00:26:12,120 --> 00:26:16,200
Oil comes out of the ground at temperatures approaching 130 degrees.

346
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:21,200
So if a hot oil pipeline were allowed to come in contact with the permafrost,

347
00:26:21,700 --> 00:26:23,240
the permafrost would melt.

348
00:26:24,140 --> 00:26:27,480
The pipeline would need to be elevated over the frozen ground

349
00:26:27,481 --> 00:26:30,500
for more than 400 of its 800 miles.

350
00:26:30,501 --> 00:26:38,040
There was also a very unique system of being able to take the heat from the pipeline and

351
00:26:38,041 --> 00:26:41,740
put it into a jacket that surrounds the pipeline.

352
00:26:41,741 --> 00:26:46,600
That heat would dissipate by vaporizing as it boiled when vented out the top so that

353
00:26:46,601 --> 00:26:51,640
you actually pulled the heat from the pipeline and didn't impact the permafrost.

354
00:26:51,641 --> 00:26:55,340
And it wasn't only the permafrost that needed protecting.

355
00:26:55,341 --> 00:26:59,580
Environmental groups had feared that animals would see the pipeline as a sort of fence

356
00:26:59,581 --> 00:27:02,160
and that migrating habits would be broken.

357
00:27:02,161 --> 00:27:05,080
Designers promised to devise a solution.

358
00:27:05,081 --> 00:27:07,720
We also had, though, wildlife to think about,

359
00:27:07,721 --> 00:27:10,120
particularly the migrating caribou herds.

360
00:27:10,121 --> 00:27:13,340
So in a number of locations, we actually elevated the pipeline

361
00:27:13,341 --> 00:27:15,320
so that the caribou could cross under it.

362
00:27:15,321 --> 00:27:18,280
In other locations, we put the pipeline underground,

363
00:27:18,281 --> 00:27:22,140
thus creating an area where the caribou could migrate through.

364
00:27:22,141 --> 00:27:26,220
Unique features that aren't found in most pipelines around the world.

365
00:27:26,221 --> 00:27:30,620
There was one other obstacle that would need to be considered, earthquakes.

366
00:27:30,621 --> 00:27:39,500
At 5.35 on Good Friday morning 1964, Alaska suffered through one of the most powerful earthquakes ever recorded.

367
00:27:40,520 --> 00:27:47,060
It registered an explosive 8.4 on the Richter scale and lasted a gut-wrenching four minutes.

368
00:27:47,820 --> 00:27:52,280
Its energy 800,000 times as great as the bomb that was dropped on Hiroshima.

369
00:27:53,460 --> 00:27:56,080
Downtown Anchorage was completely destroyed.

370
00:27:56,220 --> 00:28:03,220
If permafrost provided engineers with an unsteady base, how do you build atop one of the world's largest fault lines?

371
00:28:03,221 --> 00:28:10,220
The pipeline, as it crosses over the Denali fault, is designed to actually move 20 feet horizontally

372
00:28:10,221 --> 00:28:14,220
and as much as 5 feet vertically in the event of a seismic event.

373
00:28:14,221 --> 00:28:18,220
It's designed to withstand an 8.5 on the Richter scale.

374
00:28:18,221 --> 00:28:22,220
That bit of engineering has never been put to the test, yet.

375
00:28:22,221 --> 00:28:28,940
Yet, engineers are always being asked if the shortest route between point A and B is a straight line,

376
00:28:29,480 --> 00:28:31,800
why does the Alaskan pipeline zigzag?

377
00:28:32,440 --> 00:28:34,120
The answer is twofold.

378
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:41,320
The zigzag, which is often exaggerated by telephoto lenses, stabilizes the pipeline in the event of an earthquake.

379
00:28:41,321 --> 00:28:46,320
It also allows room for the pipe to expand and contract during temperature fluctuations.

380
00:28:46,321 --> 00:28:47,216
fluctuations.

381
00:28:47,217 --> 00:28:53,140
Living in the out of the way Arctic meant everything down to the last nut and bolt had

382
00:28:53,141 --> 00:28:56,832
to be thought of and provided for. It wasn't a glamorous life.

383
00:28:56,833 --> 00:28:58,500
Small quarters shared with a

384
00:28:58,501 --> 00:29:03,280
bunkmate, cafeteria food, and a couple of TV channels for a link with the rest of the world.

385
00:29:03,700 --> 00:29:09,580
The money was the draw for most, close to $1,100 a week, double the going rate in the States.

386
00:29:09,900 --> 00:29:14,840
When I first came to Alyeska, I went through Arctic orientation. You'd take

387
00:29:14,841 --> 00:29:18,020
the little book called Staying Alive in the Arctic.

388
00:29:18,140 --> 00:29:21,560
We all carry that little book and always carry some matches, you know,

389
00:29:21,600 --> 00:29:24,300
so if at least if you were freezing to death,

390
00:29:24,340 --> 00:29:27,560
you could burn the book Staying Alive in the Arctic, you know.

391
00:29:27,920 --> 00:29:30,340
In the course of two years of construction,

392
00:29:30,540 --> 00:29:34,560
more than 70,000 people worked on the line, 7,000 of them women.

393
00:29:35,080 --> 00:29:38,120
For a project this challenging and trying, it was a safe one.

394
00:29:38,820 --> 00:29:42,420
Amazingly, only 31 people died while building the pipeline.

395
00:29:42,421 --> 00:29:47,200
Alaskan residents pay no state income tax.

396
00:29:47,720 --> 00:29:52,500
Instead, each person over six months of age receives an oil dividend check.

397
00:29:53,080 --> 00:29:56,960
They averaged $1,000 per year during the 1990s.

398
00:29:58,840 --> 00:30:01,800
We now return to modern markets.

399
00:30:03,440 --> 00:30:11,380
Together, Thompson Pass and Attigan Pass would come to be known as the last and most dangerous miles along the pipeline's torturous route.

400
00:30:12,120 --> 00:30:15,160
Attigan and Thompson sometimes are talked about together,

401
00:30:15,380 --> 00:30:17,180
but they really are very different places,

402
00:30:17,280 --> 00:30:19,080
and the challenges are really a lot different.

403
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:21,900
They shared one thing in common, though.

404
00:30:22,220 --> 00:30:26,860
They both had to be done by the winter of 1976

405
00:30:26,861 --> 00:30:29,940
in order for us to start up in 1977.

406
00:30:30,560 --> 00:30:33,640
The pipeline was scheduled to open in less than six months,

407
00:30:33,641 --> 00:30:37,160
but a failure to finish the work in Attigan and Thompson passes

408
00:30:37,161 --> 00:30:39,860
would mean a delay of almost a full year.

409
00:30:40,800 --> 00:30:44,700
Construction during the winter months would be impossible in both places,

410
00:30:45,060 --> 00:30:49,200
so if the team missed their deadline, it would be a long wait until next summer.

411
00:30:49,720 --> 00:30:54,240
It's rainy, it's snowy, it's sleets, it's foggy.

412
00:30:54,980 --> 00:30:55,680
It's terrible.

413
00:30:57,440 --> 00:30:59,580
It's just atrocious conditions.

414
00:31:00,860 --> 00:31:05,020
The rock there is slippery rock.

415
00:31:05,021 --> 00:31:11,260
And so when it's wet or has snow on it, you just can't keep your footing.

416
00:31:12,120 --> 00:31:13,760
You slip and slide around.

417
00:31:14,260 --> 00:31:17,240
It's just a nasty place to work.

418
00:31:18,180 --> 00:31:22,960
Adigan Pass is located near the start of the line, deep within the Arctic Circle.

419
00:31:23,620 --> 00:31:24,920
It is a treacherous land.

420
00:31:25,480 --> 00:31:28,380
Avalanches occur here during the winter on a regular basis.

421
00:31:29,060 --> 00:31:33,420
At 4,000 feet, it would be the highest point that the pipeline must climb to.

422
00:31:33,421 --> 00:31:39,220
Because of the threat of snow slides, the pipeline would be encased in a mammoth concrete box.

423
00:31:39,920 --> 00:31:46,260
A trench was dug right into the mountain itself, with the granite acting as a giant concrete pore form.

424
00:31:47,640 --> 00:31:52,300
Over 100,000 cubic yards of dirt were used to backfill the giant trench,

425
00:31:52,700 --> 00:31:55,440
more than enough to fill an entire football stadium.

426
00:31:55,880 --> 00:31:57,060
The plan worked.

427
00:31:58,440 --> 00:32:02,840
Thompson Pass presented a different but just as harrowing challenge.

428
00:32:02,841 --> 00:32:07,720
The big challenge at Thompson Pass was the vertical drop and the steepness of the drop.

429
00:32:09,560 --> 00:32:20,780
The drop is so steep that conventional caterpillar tractors, cats, couldn't operate on it because their oil lubrication systems weren't even designed for them to

430
00:32:20,781 --> 00:32:23,080
operate on that steep of a slope.

431
00:32:23,760 --> 00:32:26,380
Normal machinery couldn't go up and down.

432
00:32:26,381 --> 00:32:34,040
To get around the severe 60-degree angle, the engineers devised a unique system, a makeshift aerial tramway.

433
00:32:34,820 --> 00:32:40,720
Many materials were actually flown through the air from the bottom to the top of the two-mile-long sheer wall.

434
00:32:41,760 --> 00:32:44,800
The fog is such that you really don't have good visibility.

435
00:32:45,200 --> 00:32:52,900
When the pipe was coming down the aerial tramway, it would kind of just appear, you know, like coming out of a ghosty mist.

436
00:32:52,901 --> 00:33:01,240
A little storm would blow in, and I've seen times when the welders who were actually working on the pipe were actually hanging off their safety harnesses.

437
00:33:01,300 --> 00:33:04,860
They just physically couldn't get any footing.

438
00:33:05,280 --> 00:33:12,340
As one worker put it, it was as if nature was putting up a fight, making you earn whatever you managed to wrestle out of it.

439
00:33:13,240 --> 00:33:21,940
As a manager, you had to do whatever had to be done to make sure that that Attigan Pass was complete in 1976.

440
00:33:22,900 --> 00:33:28,480
The rest was a problem, but with those two complete, it was a cakewalk.

441
00:33:29,280 --> 00:33:32,760
With both Adigan and Thompson passes successfully completed,

442
00:33:33,180 --> 00:33:36,700
it was time to hit the start button on the Alaskan pipeline.

443
00:33:37,260 --> 00:33:38,960
It was called Oil In Day.

444
00:33:39,500 --> 00:33:46,120
And now, after two years of construction, 800 miles, $9 billion, and 70,000 workers,

445
00:33:46,540 --> 00:33:51,100
the largest and most expensive construction project was finally completed.

446
00:33:51,101 --> 00:33:53,740
on June 20th, 1977

447
00:33:53,741 --> 00:33:56,320
oil finally began to flow

448
00:33:56,321 --> 00:33:58,520
through the Alaskan oil pipeline

449
00:33:58,521 --> 00:34:00,700
each time it went through a pump station

450
00:34:00,701 --> 00:34:02,740
everybody drew off some samples

451
00:34:02,741 --> 00:34:04,000
it was kind of like tapping a keg

452
00:34:04,001 --> 00:34:05,480
we all said, hey

453
00:34:05,481 --> 00:34:08,180
we got to get a sample of this

454
00:34:08,181 --> 00:34:08,881
what do we got?

455
00:34:08,900 --> 00:34:09,940
so you went into the mess hall

456
00:34:09,941 --> 00:34:11,140
and you got an old ketchup bottle

457
00:34:11,141 --> 00:34:13,600
that had just been emptied

458
00:34:13,601 --> 00:34:15,360
or a mayonnaise jar

459
00:34:15,361 --> 00:34:16,180
or something like that

460
00:34:16,181 --> 00:34:18,300
because none of us had anything to put it in

461
00:34:21,100 --> 00:34:31,600
Alaskan crude begins its 800-mile journey here at pump station number one, located on

462
00:34:31,601 --> 00:34:34,980
the edge of the Prudhoe Bay oilfield.

463
00:34:34,981 --> 00:34:40,040
And this is where the pipeline emerges for the first time from the ground to start its

464
00:34:40,041 --> 00:34:42,220
trek to Valdez.

465
00:34:42,221 --> 00:34:45,860
There isn't just one well site, there are literally a thousand.

466
00:34:45,861 --> 00:34:50,400
Several smaller feeder lines bring their supply of crude to the pump station, where they join

467
00:34:50,401 --> 00:34:54,160
together to become the Alaskan oil pipeline.

468
00:34:54,161 --> 00:34:58,420
Thanks to more advanced drilling techniques, a wellhead today is no bigger than a typical

469
00:34:58,421 --> 00:34:59,860
tool shed.

470
00:34:59,861 --> 00:35:04,860
Gone are the days of boomtown clutter and because of horizontal drilling techniques,

471
00:35:04,861 --> 00:35:08,180
a well's footprint is significantly smaller.

472
00:35:08,181 --> 00:35:14,260
Several wells can be drilled from a single spot, covering thousands of acres below.

473
00:35:14,261 --> 00:35:18,800
A metering system has been installed here to correctly calculate how much oil is being

474
00:35:18,801 --> 00:35:20,268
brought into the pipeline.

475
00:35:20,269 --> 00:35:23,880
If you're a producing oil company, it's critical to know exactly

476
00:35:23,881 --> 00:35:29,280
how much you're putting in one end, because you get paid for what comes out the other.

477
00:35:29,281 --> 00:35:33,860
At its peak of production, the pipeline was designed to send over two million barrels,

478
00:35:33,861 --> 00:35:38,325
or 84 million gallons of oil, down the pipeline every single day.

479
00:35:38,326 --> 00:35:40,180
Two giant Rolls-Royce turbo

480
00:35:40,181 --> 00:35:45,640
engines, the same as used on jumbo jets, are capable of pushing over 40,000 gallons of

481
00:35:45,641 --> 00:35:47,046
crude oil per minute.

482
00:35:47,047 --> 00:35:52,000
The crude travels between two to four miles per hour and takes almost five

483
00:35:52,001 --> 00:35:54,885
days to reach Valdez at the end of the line.

484
00:35:54,886 --> 00:35:58,360
The pump stations are also where pigs are placed into

485
00:35:58,361 --> 00:36:00,718
the pipe and sent floating downstream.

486
00:36:00,719 --> 00:36:04,380
Not the barnyard variety, but the super sophisticated type.

487
00:36:05,120 --> 00:36:09,920
No one remembers exactly how they got their name, though it might be because the old ones used to be

488
00:36:09,921 --> 00:36:13,857
made of leather and were said to squeal as they flowed down the pipe.

489
00:36:13,858 --> 00:36:15,340
Now pigs do more than just

490
00:36:15,341 --> 00:36:18,104
scrub and clean the inside walls of the pipeline.

491
00:36:18,105 --> 00:36:20,980
Today's pigs are used to help crews in preventative

492
00:36:20,981 --> 00:36:26,040
maintenance, looking for small abnormalities in the pipe system before they can turn into

493
00:36:26,041 --> 00:36:27,007
bigger problems.

494
00:36:27,008 --> 00:36:31,840
The final stop for the oil is at the marine terminal in Valdez, the northernmost

495
00:36:31,841 --> 00:36:36,200
ice-free seaport located at the top of the Prince William Sound.

496
00:36:36,201 --> 00:36:38,380
Drawing from one of its 18 giant

497
00:36:38,381 --> 00:36:45,180
holding tanks, each containing more than half a million barrels of crude, 180,000 barrels of oil

498
00:36:45,181 --> 00:36:50,410
flow through this terminus every single hour, 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

499
00:36:50,411 --> 00:36:51,260
And with four

500
00:36:51,261 --> 00:36:58,020
docks located in Valdez, up to four supersized super tankers could be entirely pumped full of

501
00:36:58,021 --> 00:36:58,932
fuel each day.

502
00:36:58,933 --> 00:37:03,820
The last critical component of the system are the super tankers themselves.

503
00:37:04,400 --> 00:37:09,000
These floating giants are responsible for delivering Alaskan oil to the rest of the

504
00:37:09,001 --> 00:37:09,701
lower 48.

505
00:37:09,702 --> 00:37:15,100
Today's oil tankers are the largest man-made objects to freely travel the surface

506
00:37:15,101 --> 00:37:22,199
of the earth. Now the biggest one that calls on Valdez is 265,000 dead weight tons.

507
00:37:22,200 --> 00:37:23,140
Just to put

508
00:37:23,141 --> 00:37:29,800
that into perspective, that's over 1.8 million barrels of oil that that tanker carries as it

509
00:37:29,801 --> 00:37:34,580
transits through Prince William Sound to market. Tankers are not a new phenomenon.

510
00:37:34,581 --> 00:37:35,281
They've been

511
00:37:35,282 --> 00:37:40,094
used since the late 1800s, and as the oil industry grew, so did they.

512
00:37:40,095 --> 00:37:41,140
Some of today's

513
00:37:41,141 --> 00:37:47,460
super tankers are over 1,500 feet long and 200 feet wide, the equivalent of over five

514
00:37:47,461 --> 00:37:54,280
football fields in length. It is a very efficient way of moving oil long distances.

515
00:37:54,281 --> 00:37:55,020
It's much

516
00:37:55,021 --> 00:37:57,030
more economic than pipelines.

517
00:37:57,031 --> 00:38:00,980
The biggest problems that we've had in the United States,

518
00:38:00,981 --> 00:38:08,020
actually with oil spills have been more from tankers having problems than we've had from pipelines.

519
00:38:12,820 --> 00:38:13,700
March 1989.

520
00:38:13,701 --> 00:38:20,340
The world watched in horror as close to 11 million gallons of crude oil hemorrhaged

521
00:38:20,341 --> 00:38:26,324
from a supertanker onto the pristine shores of Prince William Sound.

522
00:38:26,325 --> 00:38:28,260
Exxon Valdez oil spill

523
00:38:28,261 --> 00:38:35,140
was the worst nightmare that environmentalists or anybody else could have because this is a

524
00:38:35,141 --> 00:38:39,853
tremendously important and productive biological region.

525
00:38:39,854 --> 00:38:43,220
The damage was tremendous and everything

526
00:38:43,221 --> 00:38:50,180
we got out of that pipeline, in my view, is not worth the damage that that oil spill did.

527
00:38:50,181 --> 00:38:55,140
We've been on the verge of bankruptcy many times prior to the pipeline. Ironically,

528
00:38:55,141 --> 00:38:58,240
We've been bailed out by natural disasters.

529
00:38:58,900 --> 00:39:01,020
First was the earthquake in 1964.

530
00:39:01,320 --> 00:39:05,240
The port millions and billions of dollars in federal aid.

531
00:39:05,340 --> 00:39:09,080
We had another natural disaster, the Exxon Valdez oil spill.

532
00:39:09,320 --> 00:39:12,240
It pumped millions and millions of new dollars into the state.

533
00:39:12,540 --> 00:39:18,140
Terrible situation to have to rely on natural disasters for sustaining your economy.

534
00:39:18,580 --> 00:39:21,720
In the eight years since the Exxon Valdez spill,

535
00:39:21,721 --> 00:39:25,220
much has changed about the way tanker operations occur here.

536
00:39:25,760 --> 00:39:30,540
There is a fleet of over 40 sea spill ships standing by at the ready, 24 hours a day.

537
00:39:31,220 --> 00:39:35,000
There are another 300 ships kept under contract in the immediate area

538
00:39:35,001 --> 00:39:38,180
that are also a part of the emergency response team.

539
00:39:39,320 --> 00:39:43,860
Ships of every size and function, from recovery vessels capable of sucking up

540
00:39:43,861 --> 00:39:46,580
more than 2,000 barrels of oil in an hour,

541
00:39:47,080 --> 00:39:50,580
to skimmers, tugs, and emergency response boats,

542
00:39:50,581 --> 00:39:55,360
ready to deploy hundreds of thousands of feet of containment booms in minutes.

543
00:39:56,800 --> 00:40:05,980
Unlike procedures in 1989, two tugboats are now assigned to every incoming and outgoing tanker during their 70-mile transit.

544
00:40:06,700 --> 00:40:12,540
Traffic lanes have also been designated and are carefully watched by guidance and control room operators.

545
00:40:13,140 --> 00:40:17,800
The people who operate the Trans-Alaska Pipeline took the Exxon Valdez spill very seriously,

546
00:40:17,801 --> 00:40:24,040
And that spill just kind of shook our organization and our pride to its very core.

547
00:40:24,860 --> 00:40:29,320
And we feel we're in a position to respond if something like that would happen again,

548
00:40:29,400 --> 00:40:33,280
but more importantly, that we can prevent something like that from ever happening again.

549
00:40:37,400 --> 00:40:43,920
At current projections, today's pipeline is due to be shut down sometime in the next 20 years,

550
00:40:43,921 --> 00:40:47,680
once its 10 billion barrels of oil have been completely drained.

551
00:40:47,800 --> 00:40:52,800
That's close to a 40-year run as America's premier supplier of oil.

552
00:40:52,801 --> 00:40:57,800
In September 1996, another oil field was discovered alongside Prudhoe Bay.

553
00:40:57,801 --> 00:41:04,800
This one possibly equaling the oil deposit that the Alaskan pipeline draws its oil from.

554
00:41:04,801 --> 00:41:07,800
If the government approves of further drilling on the North Slope,

555
00:41:07,801 --> 00:41:13,800
oil could continue flowing from pipeline add-ons well into the new millennium.

556
00:41:13,801 --> 00:41:20,800
Conservative estimates set the value of the new find at more than 350 billion dollars.

557
00:41:20,801 --> 00:41:28,800
But as in the past, environmentalists question the need to go into one of the world's last, most pristine environments.

558
00:41:28,801 --> 00:41:36,800
Known as ANWR, the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge, it is 19 million federally protected acres of land.

559
00:41:36,801 --> 00:41:44,240
Is there no point at which we will not stop and say, wait a minute, this place cannot be damaged.

560
00:41:44,241 --> 00:41:45,840
This place is too important.

561
00:41:46,680 --> 00:41:49,620
If we don't hold ourselves back here, where will we?

562
00:41:50,260 --> 00:41:55,120
We might as well start drilling in Yosemite, because no place is sacred, and it's pointless.

563
00:41:55,460 --> 00:41:57,460
It's stupid, and we shouldn't do it.

564
00:41:57,461 --> 00:42:07,920
After 20 years of operation, what is the scorecard on the Alaskan pipeline today?

565
00:42:08,720 --> 00:42:12,560
Technologically, the pipeline has exceeded anyone's expectations,

566
00:42:13,320 --> 00:42:16,100
pushing Arctic engineering to an entirely new level.

567
00:42:16,780 --> 00:42:22,480
Spills have occurred on the pipeline itself, but they have been minor, measured in gallons, not in barrels.

568
00:42:22,481 --> 00:42:27,480
Environmentally, its short-term impact seems to be minimal.

569
00:42:27,481 --> 00:42:31,480
The pipeline seems to have lived up to its designers' promises.

570
00:42:31,481 --> 00:42:36,480
In the long term, only time will tell if human activity on such a massive scale

571
00:42:36,481 --> 00:42:40,480
brings damage to such a sensitive land.

572
00:42:40,481 --> 00:42:45,480
Sociologically, the pipeline has been a provider of opportunities, jobs, new schools,

573
00:42:45,481 --> 00:42:48,480
and hospitals for Alaskan residents,

574
00:42:48,481 --> 00:42:52,480
and a regained independence for America from foreign influences.

575
00:42:53,480 --> 00:42:58,080
When and if the day ever comes that there is no further need for an Alaskan pipeline,

576
00:42:58,580 --> 00:43:04,320
its charter provides for its complete removal and restoration of the land to its original condition.

577
00:43:05,300 --> 00:43:11,220
Some 20 years after its completion, the Alaskan oil pipeline still generates strong feelings.

578
00:43:12,960 --> 00:43:16,500
You sort of have to close your eyes and think about this thing.

579
00:43:16,501 --> 00:43:20,940
Cutting like a knife across the heart of the last great place left on the continent,

580
00:43:21,180 --> 00:43:26,660
that is perhaps a psychological impact whose importance I don't think we begin to understand yet.

581
00:43:27,160 --> 00:43:29,860
It would have been so much worse without the pipeline.

582
00:43:30,420 --> 00:43:33,900
There was a psychological effect on the American character.

583
00:43:34,980 --> 00:43:36,860
Jimmy Carter called it a malaise.

584
00:43:37,400 --> 00:43:39,660
People felt that we were losing control.

585
00:43:40,340 --> 00:43:44,860
And then the good old American know-how and engineers built this enormous pipeline

586
00:43:44,861 --> 00:43:46,400
in a fairly short time.

587
00:43:46,840 --> 00:43:48,880
And I think it helped to show a spirit

588
00:43:48,881 --> 00:43:51,180
that we are capable people,

589
00:43:51,560 --> 00:43:53,020
that we can overcome adversity.

590
00:43:53,620 --> 00:43:54,800
Well, it was a memorial to everybody

591
00:43:54,801 --> 00:43:56,600
who worked on the pipeline, and that's in Valdez.

592
00:43:57,360 --> 00:43:58,520
It's really pretty moving.

593
00:43:58,860 --> 00:44:00,760
And one day I went by the monument there,

594
00:44:00,980 --> 00:44:02,660
and we'd had a tremendous snow.

595
00:44:03,620 --> 00:44:07,420
And the snow was right up to the necks of the people,

596
00:44:08,460 --> 00:44:09,880
you know, the statues there.

597
00:44:10,780 --> 00:44:12,820
It was kind of indicative of what it took

598
00:44:12,821 --> 00:44:13,760
to build the pipeline.

599
00:44:13,761 --> 00:44:17,440
I mean, it looked like those guys were sitting there going like this, you know, trying to,

600
00:44:17,441 --> 00:44:22,040
trying to, you know, just stay above water, if you will, you know, and it was perfect.

601
00:44:22,180 --> 00:44:22,880
It was perfect.

602
00:44:22,881 --> 00:44:26,400
It made me think that that's what building the pipeline was all about, struggling against

603
00:44:26,401 --> 00:44:27,820
those elements and overcoming them.

